 A browser frame of the REAL route, driven by the ported render.

 This replaces poc/Scene, which placed its own scenery by hand because almost
 nothing was ported when it was written. Nothing here is placed by hand. The road,
 its corners, the conifers, the intersection towers, the guard rails and the pond
 all come out of `Safari chapter World` -- the same nineteen segments graded at
 3,822 values -- mapped by Render's chain and ordered by Render's depth sort.

 IT IS STILL A POC, AND HERE IS EXACTLY WHAT IS NOT REAL ABOUT IT.

 THE ANIMALS ARE HERE NOW. Their art is `Safari chapter EmojiStills`, generated
 from the game's own baked table by harness/bake_emoji_stills.py rather than
 hand-carried, and `Safari chapter Critter` places it. One still per species, 129
 polygons in all -- nothing per-frame is baked; the animals are static billboards
 that scale with distance, and every bit of motion in this scene is computed.

 THE BULL DRAWS FLAT. It is the only Fluent Color animal and 40 of its 43 polygons
 carry a 2-stop gradient, which paint.zig writes as wire tags 5 and 6 that Paint
 does not model yet, so the generated table flattens each to its first stop.

 The cat still draws nothing: its flipbook is a second, bigger table. The truck is
 the same -- it drives, and its body reaches chain machinery that is ported now, so
 it is unblocked rather than missing.

 The rider is not simulated. `Safari chapter Rider` is ported and graded, but it is
 a step function over a mutable state and the shim's scrub is PURE -- frame-at (u)
 has to answer from u alone. So u walks the route by arc length at a constant
 speed, dead centre of the lane, looking straight down it. No lean, no gaze, no
 throttle. Driving the real rider means giving the shim state to carry, and that is
 a different change.

 The backdrop heading is BLENDED ACROSS EACH CORNER rather than taken from the
 rider. A segment's north-heading is a step function -- it jumps by the whole turn
 at the joint -- and the mountains are drawn from it, so taken raw the entire
 horizon would snap sideways as the rider crosses. The real game does not have this
 problem because rider.zig turns him continuously. Blending over the last stretch
 of each segment is a stand-in for that, and it is the one number on this page that
 is invented rather than ported.

 We say:

Section: The Scrub

 u runs 0..1 and the shim advances it. Everything on the page is a function of it.

 The sunset clock runs over the sweep, so a full pass takes the scene from day to
 dusk. It is the same clock Sky grades: sky-color, horizon-color and the sun's
 placement all read it.

  scene-step-at : Real -> Real
  scene-step-at (u) = 3600.0 + 1400.0 * u

 HOW FAR u MOVES IN ONE FRAME, AND IT IS THE ROUTE THAT DECIDES, not a constant
 picked to look right. rider.zig measures speed in METRES PER FRAME -- v-base 0.3,
 v-max 2.5 -- so a frame covers about two metres once he is up to speed and
 braking for corners. u is arc length over the whole course, so one frame is that
 distance divided by the course.

 The scrub had a flat 1/900, which over a 7.5 km course is 8.4 m a frame: more
 than three times the rider's top speed, and it looked it. Deriving it from
 v-max means the page cannot drift away from the game's own pace again, and a
 route change moves it automatically.

  drive-speed : Real
  drive-speed = v-max * 0.8

  u-per-step : Real
  u-per-step = drive-speed / (course-length build-world * 0.995)

Section: Where On The Route

 THE WORLD IS THREADED, NOT REFERENCED, and that is a performance fix rather than
 a style choice. A nullary Codex binding emits as a zig FUNCTION, not a memoised
 constant -- `segs = build-world` became `fn segs() { return build_world(); }` --
 so every mention rebuilt all nineteen segments with their trees, herds and cat
 placements. `pos-from` mentions it twice per loop iteration, so one frame rebuilt
 the world hundreds of times: 270 ms a frame, about four per second. Passing it
 down as a parameter, the way every graded chapter in the port already does,
 builds it once. See PORTING_NOTES B13.

 How far along the whole course, in metres. Stopping just short of the end keeps
 the walk below inside the last segment rather than falling off it.

  drive-dist-at : List Segment, Real -> Real
  drive-dist-at (w) (u) = course-length w * u * 0.995

  RoutePos = record {
    seg : Integer,
    along : Real
  }

  pos-from : List Segment, Real, Integer -> RoutePos
  pos-from (w) (dist) (i) =
    if i + 1 >= list-length w then RoutePos { seg = i, along = dist }
    else if dist < (list-at w i).length then RoutePos { seg = i, along = dist }
    else pos-from w (dist - (list-at w i).length) (i + 1)

  drive-pos-at : List Segment, Real -> RoutePos
  drive-pos-at (w) (u) = pos-from w (drive-dist-at w u) 0

 THE BLENDED HEADING, and the reason it exists is in this chapter's opening. A
 segment's north-heading jumps by the whole exit turn at the joint; blending into
 the next one over the last stretch keeps the mountains from snapping sideways.

  turn-blend-dist : Real
  turn-blend-dist = 120.0

  heading-of : List Segment, RoutePos -> Real
  heading-of (w) (p) =
    let sg = list-at w p.seg
    in if p.seg + 1 >= list-length w then sg.north-heading
      else blend-heading sg (list-at w (p.seg + 1)) p.along

  blend-heading : Segment, Segment, Real -> Real
  blend-heading (sg) (nx) (along) =
    let left = sg.length - along
    in if left >= turn-blend-dist then sg.north-heading
      else sg.north-heading + (nx.north-heading - sg.north-heading) * (turn-blend-dist - left) / turn-blend-dist

  heading-in : List Segment, Real -> Real
  heading-in (w) (u) = heading-of w (drive-pos-at w u)

  scene-heading-at : Real -> Real
  scene-heading-at (u) = heading-in build-world u

 The shim reads the sun straight off Sky, as the old scene did.

  scene-sun-at : Real -> SunPos
  scene-sun-at (u) = sun-pos (heading-in build-world u) (scene-step-at u) focal camera-w

Section: The Pose

 Dead centre of the lane, looking straight down it. across and yaw are the two
 numbers the rider would own, and both are zero here.

  pose-in : List Segment, Real -> Pose
  pose-in (w) (u) =
    let p = drive-pos-at w u
    in Pose { along = p.along, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, hw = (list-at w p.seg).width / 2.0 }

Section: Drawing What Is Sorted

 The depth order Render produced, painted far to near. Three of the six kinds draw:
 trees, towers and rails. Billboards and the truck body reach the baked frames and
 render nothing, which is why the frame has correctly-ordered holes rather than
 misplaced animals.

  tree-fwd-of : List TreeItem, Integer -> Real
  tree-fwd-of (ts) (i) = (list-at ts i).fwd

 How many trees stand strictly nearer than this one. Only the closest four get a
 round shaded trunk, and since a roadside pair shares one depth, "fewer than four
 strictly closer" is the nearest two pairs.

  closer-count : List TreeItem, Real, Integer -> Integer
  closer-count (ts) (f) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ts then 0
    else if (list-at ts i).fwd < f then 1 + closer-count ts f (i + 1)
    else closer-count ts f (i + 1)

  crown-shade-of : Real -> Real
  crown-shade-of (fwd) =
    let raw = 1.0 - fwd / crown-shade-dist
    in if raw < 0.0 then 0.0 else (if raw > 1.0 then 1.0 else raw)

  draw-one-tree : List TreeItem, Integer, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-one-tree (ts) (i) (cf) =
    let t = list-at ts i
    in tree-draw t.right t.fwd t.height t.color cf camera-w (closer-count ts t.fwd 0 < 4) (t.fwd < detail-dist) (crown-shade-of t.fwd)

 A tower's four base corners are re-derived from its yaw at draw time and mapped
 through the frame that owns it, which is why the collector kept the recipe rather
 than the points.

  tower-base : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, TowerItem, Integer -> List RiderPt
  tower-base (w) (ch) (pose) (tw) (k) =
    if k >= 4 then []
    else [map-pt w ch pose tw.map (base-corner-ax k tw.a0 tw.x0 tw.yaw).a (base-corner-ax k tw.a0 tw.x0 tw.yaw).x]
         & tower-base w ch pose tw (k + 1)

  draw-one-tower : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, TowerItem, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-one-tower (w) (ch) (pose) (tw) (cf) (step) =
    draw-flat (tower-base w ch pose tw 0) (map-pt w ch pose tw.map tw.a0 tw.x0) cf camera-w (step + tw.off)

  draw-one-critter : Billboard, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-one-critter (b) (cf) = critter-draw b.right b.fwd b.height b.cp b.face-right cf camera-w

  draw-item : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Collected, Item, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-item (w) (ch) (pose) (c) (it) (cf) (step) =
   when it.kind
    is KTree -> draw-one-tree c.trees it.i cf
    is KTower -> draw-one-tower w ch pose (list-at c.towers it.i) cf step
    is KRail -> rail-draw-poly (list-at c.rails it.i) cf camera-w
    is KCow -> draw-one-critter (list-at c.cows it.i) cf
    is KCat -> []
    is KTruck -> []

  draw-order : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Collected, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  draw-order (w) (ch) (pose) (c) (cf) (step) (i) =
    if i >= list-length c.order then []
    else (draw-item w ch pose c (list-at c.order i) cf step) & draw-order w ch pose c cf step (i + 1)

Section: The Frame

 Backdrop, then the floor, then everything that stands up in depth order. That is
 render.zig's own sequence.

  frame-at : Real -> List DrawCmd
  frame-at (u) = frame-in build-world u

  frame-in : List Segment, Real -> List DrawCmd
  frame-in (w) (u) =
    let p = drive-pos-at w u
    in let pose = pose-in w u
    in let step = scene-step-at u
    in let ch = build-chain w p.seg
    in (draw (heading-in w u) (sun-set-fraction step) focal camera-w)
       & (frame-ground w p.seg pose focal camera-w)
       & (draw-order w ch pose (collect w p.seg pose focal p.along 0.0 0.0) focal step 0)

  frame : List DrawCmd
  frame = frame-at 0.0

Section: Driving The Real Rider

 THE MOTION IS NO LONGER INVENTED. Everything above this section answers from a
 scrub `u` at a constant speed, which was a stand-in for the physics while the
 shim had nowhere to keep state. `Safari chapter Rider` is ported and graded at
 199 values one step from a shared state, so the page can simply run it -- and
 measuring what it actually does is what made clear that the stand-in was wrong.
 Over the first 4,000 frames the real rider averages 1.18 m/frame and ranges from
 0.53 on the tight second leg to 1.75 on the long sixth. The scrub ran a flat 2.0.

 What driving the real rider fixes, all at once and all as consequences rather
 than as separate patches:

   the SPEED is the game's, corner by corner, instead of one average;
   there IS acceleration and braking, which a constant cannot have;
   the LEAN comes out of it -- tilt is a field on the state, nonzero on 1,552 of
     those 4,000 frames and reaching 17.6 degrees, and the page simply never asked;
   the HEADING is the rider's own, so the blended stand-in above -- the one number
     on this page that was invented -- is no longer needed.

 The head turn is the rider's gaze plus a fraction of his lean, exactly as
 safari.zig folds it before building the pose: the whole view swings, backdrop
 included.

  head-yaw-frac : Real
  head-yaw-frac = 0.15

  view-yaw-for : RiderState -> Real
  view-yaw-for (s) = s.gaze-yaw + head-yaw-frac * s.tilt

 The sunset clock runs on ROUTE DISTANCE rather than a frame count, so the sweep
 from day to dusk covers the course however fast the rider takes it.

  step-for : List Segment, RiderState -> Real
  step-for (w) (s) =
    3600.0 + 1400.0 * route-distance w s.segment s.along / course-length w

  heading-for : RiderState -> Real
  heading-for (s) = s.heading + view-yaw-for s

  sun-for : List Segment, RiderState -> SunPos
  sun-for (w) (s) = sun-pos (heading-for s) (step-for w s) focal camera-w

  pose-for : List Segment, RiderState -> Pose
  pose-for (w) (s) =
    Pose { along = s.along, across = s.across, yaw = s.yaw + view-yaw-for s, hw = (list-at w s.segment).width / 2.0 }

  frame-for : List Segment, RiderState -> List DrawCmd
  frame-for (w) (s) =
    let pose = pose-for w s
    in let step = step-for w s
    in let ch = build-chain w s.segment
    in (draw (heading-for s) (sun-set-fraction step) focal camera-w)
       & (frame-ground w s.segment pose focal camera-w)
       & (draw-order w ch pose (collect w s.segment pose focal s.along s.v 0.0) focal step 0)
